,title,link,text,language,family
0,Jarai people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jarai_people,"The Jarai language is a member of the Malayo-Polynesian branch of the Austronesian language family. It is related to the Cham language of central Vietnam and Cambodia and the Malayo-Polynesian languages of Indonesia, Malaysia, Madagascar, Philippines and other Pacific Islands such as Hawaii and New Zealand.",Jarai,Austronesian
1,Jarai language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jarai_language,"Jarai (/ dʒəˈraɪ /; Vietnamese: tiếng Gia Rai or Tiếng Jrai; Khmer: ចារ៉ាយ, Charay [caːraːj]) is a Malayo-Polynesian language spoken by the Jarai people of Vietnam and Cambodia.",Jarai,Austronesian
2,Traditional Jarai Music by Hip Ksor (Ksor Hip Adôh Tơlơi Jrai Hơđăp),https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_NVTq2s3qEc,"As we play guitars, Hip Ksor performs a heartfelt traditional Jarai song about our ancestral homeland. His song recalls the Apa River, Ayun River, the Central Highlands mountains and forests, and ...",Jarai,Austronesian
3,Jarai Traditional Song by Hip Ksor | Montagnard Music from Vietnam,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wUVS62xTGi4,"Thank you for watching! Please like, comment, and subscribe to the Montagnard Channel for more videos about Jarai and Montagnard culture, music, language, and history.",Jarai,Austronesian
4,"Jarai Language - Structure, Writing & Alphabet - MustGo",https://www.mustgo.com/worldlanguages/jarai/,"Jarai (also spelled as Jrai, Cho-Rai, Chor, Djarai, Chrai, Gia Rai, Gio Rai, Jörai, and Mthur) is a Chamic language in the Malayo-Polynesian branch of the Austronesian language family (Ethnologue). Jarai is spoken by approximately 318,000 Jarai people, predominantly in the Highlands Gia Lai province of Vietnam. It is also spoken by about 20,000 people in Cambodia, by over 3,000 people in the ...",Jarai,Austronesian
5,Gong Culture of Southeast Asia: Jarai (Vietnam),https://the-concrete.bandcamp.com/album/gong-culture-of-southeast-asia-jarai-vietnam,"Funeral Music The Jarai people live mainly in Gia Lai Province, as well as in the western part of Kon Tum and the northern part of Dak Lak provinces. They belong to the Malayo-Polynesian branch of the Austronesian language family and are considered among the earliest inhabitants of Vietnam's Central Highlands.",Jarai,Austronesian
6,"Jarai alphabet, pronunciation and language - Omniglot",https://www.omniglot.com/writing/jarai.htm,"Jarai (Jơrai) Jarai is a member of the Chamic branch of Malayo-Polynesian languages spoken in parts of Vietnam and Cambodia by about 332,557 people. It is spoken in the Central Highlands of Vietnam, particularly in Gia Lai and Kon Tum provinces, and also in Đắk Lắk province, and in Ratanakiri province in Cambodia.",Jarai,Austronesian
7,Jarai people traditional music - NativeHistory.Info,https://www.nativehistory.info/jarai-people-traditional-music/,"The Jarai people traditional music is a vibrant, living testament to the rich cultural heritage of an indigenous community deeply connected to its land and spiritual beliefs.",Jarai,Austronesian
8,Music of the Buffalo Sacrifice Ceremony of the Jarai,https://vincenzodellaratta.bandcamp.com/album/music-of-the-buffalo-sacrifice-ceremony-of-the-jarai,"about This collection features a selection of gong music performed during the Buffalo Sacrifice ceremony of the Jarai, the largest ethnic group among the indigenous peoples of the Central Highlands of Vietnam (Tây Nguyên). The Jarai language belongs to the Austronesian family, and the group is divided into five subgroups: the northern Hơdrung, Arap, and Tơ Buan, and the southern Čor and M ...",Jarai,Austronesian
9,The Jarai People: The Beauty of Central Highlands Culture,https://volotour.com/the-jarai-people-the-beauty-of-central-highlands-culture/,"The Jarai language is a member of the Malayo-Polynesian branch of the Austronesian language family. It is related to the Cham language of central Vietnam and Cambodia and the Malayo-Polynesian languages of Indonesia, Malaysia, Madagascar, Philippines and other Pacific Islands such as Hawaii and New Zealand.",Jarai,Austronesian
